WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
THINKPAD DOMINATION
Do you know what the best selling electronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:09.560
of all time is? Well if you guessed the iPhone or&nbsp;
the Samsung Galaxy, then you’re probably right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:15.400
but that’s not all that surprising. iPhones and&nbsp;
Galaxies are basically ubiquitous around the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:22.400
having sold billions of units. But something that&nbsp;
might be surprising is how many Thinkpads Lenovo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.840
has managed to sell. Over the years, Lenovo&nbsp;
has sold a whopping 200 million Thinkpads. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:35.200
perspective, that makes Lenovo the world's largest&nbsp;
PC maker by unit sales, a title that they’ve held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.800
on and off for decades. And what makes this&nbsp;
achievement even more impressive is that Lenovo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.760
breaks basically every rule you can think of with&nbsp;
the Thinkpad. For starters, the Thinkpad doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.920
look all that impressive. It’s not the sleekest&nbsp;
laptop or thinnest laptop like Apple is trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.800
create. Nor is it the bulkiest and most overkill&nbsp;
laptop like Asus is trying to create. Heck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.400
Thinkpads still have those red TrackPoints in the&nbsp;
middle of the keyboard as if we’re living in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:11.400
early 2000s. In fact, Lenovo hasn’t really changed&nbsp;
the design all that much since the Thinkpad was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.240
introduced way back in 1993. Yet, none of&nbsp;
this has stopped the Thinkpad because while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:23.880
the Thinkpad might not be the coolest laptop, it&nbsp;
does exactly what it promises. It’s practical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.120
functional, reliable, and offers phenomenal&nbsp;
value. From a grounded perspective, these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.560
exactly what any consumer should be looking for.&nbsp;
But as we’ve seen with the smartphone market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.360
this doesn’t always hold up. In fact, some of the&nbsp;
worst selling smartphones are the most practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:46.320
smartphones like the Google Pixel and HTC&nbsp;
smartphones. Meanwhile, people tend to buy iPhones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.040
year after year even if nothing changes simply&nbsp;
because the iPhone branding and marketing are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.080
so powerful. Yet, within the laptop market, Lenovo&nbsp;
has been able to rise above all the hype and carve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:02.520
out a massive segment for themselves. In fact,&nbsp;
many would argue that if you want all the perks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.840
a Macbook without the Apple tax or Apple’s antics,&nbsp;
you get a Thinkpad. So, here’s the story of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:24.216
of the last practical electronics that’s still&nbsp;
killing it in the year 2024: the Lenovo Thinkpad.

00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:26.320
UNPARALLELED EFFICIENCY:
Taking a look back, the story of Lenovo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.400
takes us back to November 1, 1984 to a group of&nbsp;
Chinese engineers led by Liu Chuanzhi and Danny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.240
Lui. Originally, the company was called “Legend”&nbsp;
and the team was focused on importing TVs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.120
but after this effort fell flat, Legend would&nbsp;
pivot to the rapidly growing PC market. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.760
wasn’t all that easy either. Legend didn’t exactly&nbsp;
have the technical knowhow or resources to bring a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.960
PC to market and scale production. As such, they&nbsp;
opted to fulfill background functions with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.320
market. For example, they started off as a quality&nbsp;
checker for existing computer brands. They would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.440
also go on to create a circuit board that allowed&nbsp;
IBM compatible PCs to process Chinese characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.360
But while these efforts were enough to run a small&nbsp;
local business, Liu had much bigger ambitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:19.120
for Legend. And to even have a chance at those&nbsp;
ambitions, Liu first had to hire a much bigger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.440
knowledgeable team that could handle a task that&nbsp;
was as daunting as bringing a PC to market. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:30.200
such, in May of 1988, Liu would pull the trigger&nbsp;
and place a job ad on the front page of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:36.520
Youth News. They only had enough money to hire 16&nbsp;
people but given the novelty of the PC industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:42.811
Legend would receive an unprecedented number&nbsp;
of applications. 500 to be precise for a no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.811 --> 00:03:47.720
name computer company doing quality checks.&nbsp;
Liu decided to take advantage of the interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.000
and move forward with as many applicants as&nbsp;
possible. 280 would be given a written tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.080
120 would be interviewed in person, and 58 would&nbsp;
be given offers even though they could only afford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.360
16. And before they knew it, they would end up&nbsp;
with a staff including 18 people with graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.960
degrees, 37 people with undergraduate degrees,&nbsp;
and 3 students. One of these hires was a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:16.600
named Yang Yuanqing who would go on to be the&nbsp;
CEO of Lenovo but that was still a long ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.560
away. In the meantime, Legend had to figure&nbsp;
out how to support a workforce they couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.520
afford. This mainly consisted of taking on a&nbsp;
bunch of debt and living on scraps. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.440
money was so tight that Liu and his coworkers&nbsp;
wouldn’t even take cheap public transportation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:38.200
opting to walk everywhere. But, from Liu’s&nbsp;
perspective, this was all more than worth it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.720
because he was spending all of this money&nbsp;
on engineering talent and R&amp;D, not flashy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.520
office buildings or corporate retreats. And that&nbsp;
brings us into really the key advantage of Lenovo:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.120
unparalleled efficiency. Lenovo is able to&nbsp;
run a leaner and cheaper business than even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.160
most of their Chinese counterparts. And when&nbsp;
we’re talking about an industry such as PCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.720
which have been largely commoditized over the&nbsp;
past few decades, this is critical. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:12.200
the average net margin for computers and&nbsp;
peripherals currently stands at just 3.42%. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.160
coming out on top in this cutthroat market&nbsp;
is very much a test of efficiency and grit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:22.480
an area in which Lenovo has largely excelled. In&nbsp;
fact, Lenovo regularly boasts net margins that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:30.400
are far slimmer than even 3.42%. They usually&nbsp;
hover somewhere around just 1.5%. So, if you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.440
ever wondered how Lenovo is able to offer such&nbsp;
value packed devices, well there’s your answer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:42.640
All the money you’re spending is directly going to&nbsp;
just R&amp;D and parts. Virtually none of it goes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.400
fancy redesigns or advertising efforts. In fact,&nbsp;
even today, Lenovo only spends $100 million per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:55.520
year on advertising. For perspective, Dell spends&nbsp;
over 10 times that every single year. This tunnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.480
visioned focus of cutting costs everywhere except&nbsp;
for where they really matter is what has allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:14.946
Lenovo to launch such value packed computers&nbsp;
which brings into the company’s first computer.

00:06:14.946 --> 00:06:17.280
MAKING A NAME:
Starting in 1990, Legend would begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.520
manufacturing and selling their own computers.&nbsp;
One of their biggest hits early on was the KT8920&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.760
mainframe computer. A lot of their models from&nbsp;
the 1990s were rather tacky and had odd quirks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:36.600
but back in the day, people loved these quirks.&nbsp;
For example, in their 1998 Tianxi computer, Legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.120
included a physical button who’s whole purpose&nbsp;
was to just connect the computer to the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:47.920
and open the default web browser. Obviously,&nbsp;
such buttons seem rather out of place in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.520
but back in the 1990s, people loved it. Most&nbsp;
of Legend’s consumers were first time consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.760
buyers and having a dedicated button to access&nbsp;
the internet was extremely helpful. In fact, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:03.880
Tianxi would go on to be the best selling computer&nbsp;
in Chinese history at the time, selling 1,000,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:09.400
units in 2000 alone. Such successes allowed Legend&nbsp;
to crawl out of their debt hole from the late 80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:14.840
and even host a successful IPO, but they never&nbsp;
forgot about their roots and what actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.200
them successful. And as such, they would pour&nbsp;
all of their profits into improving production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:25.280
standards and R&amp;D which would eventually allow&nbsp;
them to launch their first laptop in 1996. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.800
end of the 1990s, Legend would become a household&nbsp;
name, controlling 43% of the Chinese market. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:37.800
outside of China, Legend was a nobody which&nbsp;
brings us into the Thinkpad. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.280
most people wouldn’t bat an eye if you said that&nbsp;
Lenovo created the Thinkpad given that Lenovo and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:49.160
Thinkpad have been synonymous for so long now,&nbsp;
but the Thinkpad wasn’t Lenovo’s creation. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:54.800
was actually IBM’s creation. Shifting back&nbsp;
to the early 1990s, Toshiba and Compaq had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.720
come out with portable computers that they called&nbsp;
notebooks and IBM wanted a piece of the pie. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.680
they would deploy their vast international&nbsp;
resources to create a competing notebook that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:11.480
they would brand with their famous slogan “THINK”.&nbsp;
IBM would launch the ThinkPad in October of 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:17.640
and it was a great capable portable computer but&nbsp;
it was by no means sales driver largely due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:25.560
price. The original ThinkPad for example started&nbsp;
at $2,750 which is the same $5,700 today. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:32.760
it went up to $4,800 or $10,000 today. As such,&nbsp;
the Thinkpad really only appealed to businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:38.520
universities, and the government. Fun fact, in&nbsp;
1998, the ThinkPad would become the first computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:43.920
to be certified for use on the ISS, and to this&nbsp;
day, it is still the only computer that’s allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:50.080
on the ISS. But aside from this fun fact, the&nbsp;
ThinkPad didn’t really have much else going for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.280
it. At the end of the day, it was a niche highly&nbsp;
priced product that was completely out of reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:01.040
for the average person, but that’s exactly how&nbsp;
IBM liked it. They didn’t want to get into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.440
trenches and compete in a cutthroat market. They&nbsp;
simply weren’t all that good at that. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:11.800
that’s why they got crushed in the PC market&nbsp;
despite pioneering the way. They simply weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.000
able to compete against cheaper alternatives&nbsp;
from Dell, HP, and Compaq. Laptops, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:23.520
were a high margin industry where the excelled,&nbsp;
wait nevermind, that didn’t last forever. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.920
turn of the century, laptops would also become&nbsp;
a highly competitive industry with low margins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:34.680
And by the end of 2004, IBM wanted no part of&nbsp;
it. So, they would decide to sell ThinkPad to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:41.520
one of their largest suppliers, Legend, for $1.75&nbsp;
billion. From Legend’s perspective, this was their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.360
golden opportunity to make an international&nbsp;
name for themselves. So, they wouldn’t waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:59.120
anytime in rebranding themselves as Lenovo and&nbsp;
kickstarting a whole new era for the ThinkPad.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.960
MARKET DOMINATION:
Lenovo’s approach to ThinkPad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:07.200
was pretty simply and straightforward. The game&nbsp;
plan was to simply apply Lenovo’s unparalleled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:13.440
efficiency to the laptop market while retaining&nbsp;
all of the best features of IBM. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:19.320
Lenovo very much adopted the transparency of&nbsp;
IBM. Now of course, you could argue that IBM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:24.760
isn’t all the transparent to begin with but&nbsp;
for Chinese standards, Lenovo made massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:30.560
strides forward. For instance, Lenovo adopted the&nbsp;
American standard of releasing quarterly financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.680
reports even though they were only required to&nbsp;
report twice a year back home. They also created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:41.040
committees for audits and compensation. And Lenovo&nbsp;
even go on to host the first investor relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:46.360
conference in mainland China. But, not only was&nbsp;
Lenovo go on to impress investors but they also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:51.720
impressed a lot of computer enthusiasts who&nbsp;
swear by the ThinkPad. From their perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.240
the ThinkPad was a no brainer. You basically&nbsp;
got a workhorse computer that used to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:02.640
status symbol amongst business users for an&nbsp;
affordable price thanks to Lenovo. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.280
the status of ThinkPads have very much worn off&nbsp;
but the practicality hasn’t. While the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.840
the PC industry has trended towards “the Apple&nbsp;
Way”, producing computers that look sleek but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.880
are a nightmare to repair or upgrade, Lenovo&nbsp;
has stuck to their tried and true approach:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:23.440
essentially producing a no thrills, pro consumer&nbsp;
MacBook. Now of course, this doesn’t appeal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:29.040
everyone, but for the people it does appeal&nbsp;
to, they would choose nothing else. Over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.320
Lenovo has tried to apply this approach to&nbsp;
other industries like the smartphone industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:39.120
For example, they bought Motorola from Google for&nbsp;
$2.91 billion in 2014 in an effort to break into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.560
the smartphone market. But, Lenovo would figure&nbsp;
out the hard way that smartphones are still very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.400
much a status symbol. And even someone like&nbsp;
Google isn’t able to carve out market share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:55.040
using a practical phone. But, while smartphones&nbsp;
didn’t work out in their favor, Lenovo has managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:01.120
to find success in other no thrills industries&nbsp;
like the server market. In 2014, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.200
Lenovo would purchase yet another IBM business,&nbsp;
their server business for $2.3 billion. And today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:13.360
Lenovo stands as the 4th largest server maker&nbsp;
in the world controlling 6.2% of the market. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:20.160
of this has allowed Lenovo to grow to as much as&nbsp;
$70 billion per year in revenue. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.760
that makes them a top 200 company in terms of&nbsp;
revenue. Given their meager profit margins though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:31.200
this doesn’t even translate to $1 billion worth of&nbsp;
profit today, but hey is that really something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:37.560
complain about? After all, that’s what makes&nbsp;
Lenovo the last practical electronics maker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:42.520
While Lenovo has managed to maintain large demand&nbsp;
in the modern era, the same cannot be said about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:47.480
other practical electronics makers like Sony and&nbsp;
Panasonic. Check out this video to learn more.

